@question {{read_notation_id}} | {{read_notation_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{read_notation_objective}}
@given {{read_notation_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | What exact horizontal coordinate must the original equation have?
{{read_notation_choices_1}}
@feedback 12 | This reverses the required coordinate sign. Substituting {{read_notation_opposite}} gives ({{read_notation_a}})({{read_notation_opposite}})+({{read_notation_b}})={{read_notation_wrong_sign_lhs}}, not {{read_notation_c}}. Keep the signed divisor when isolating cosine.
@feedback 13 | This coordinate is too large by 1/2. It gives ({{read_notation_a}})({{read_notation_shifted}})+({{read_notation_b}})={{read_notation_shifted_lhs}}, not {{read_notation_c}}. Subtract the constant on both sides, then divide both sides.
@after {{read_notation_after_1}}
@wrong A coordinate is a number, not an angle. Undo the added constant and the nonzero multiplier in that order.
@why Here A={{read_notation_a}}, B={{read_notation_b}} and C={{read_notation_c}}. Subtracting B gives ({{read_notation_a}})cosine(theta)={{read_notation_c}}-({{read_notation_b}})={{read_notation_rhs}}. Dividing by {{read_notation_a}} gives cosine(theta)={{read_notation_rhs}}/({{read_notation_a}})={{read_notation_level}}. Check the coordinate in the original: ({{read_notation_a}})({{read_notation_level}})+({{read_notation_b}})={{read_notation_c}}. Multiplying and adding back reverse the moves, so every angle at this coordinate, and only such an angle, solves the original.
@end

@question {{worked_check_id}} | {{worked_check_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{worked_check_objective}}
@given {{worked_check_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate. The displayed theta value is one known solution, not a restriction to that angle.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which other permitted angle has the same cosine coordinate?
{{worked_check_choices_1}}
@feedback 11 | {{worked_check_known}} is the known angle already supplied. It checks the equation, but repeating it does not supply the other angle requested.
@feedback 13 | At {{worked_check_wrong_angle}}, cosine is {{worked_check_wrong_coordinate}}, not {{worked_check_level}}. A quarter-turn changes this coordinate; it is not the required reflection.
@after {{worked_check_after_1}}
@wrong Find a distinct point with the same horizontal coordinate, not a repeat of the known point or a quarter-turn.
@why Subtracting {{worked_check_b}} and dividing by {{worked_check_a}} gives cosine(theta)=({{worked_check_c}}-({{worked_check_b}}))/({{worked_check_a}})={{worked_check_rhs}}/({{worked_check_a}})={{worked_check_level}}. Reflect the known {{worked_check_known}} across the horizontal axis: 2pi-{{worked_check_known}}={{worked_check_reflected_raw}}; adding {{worked_check_reflection_shift}} gives {{worked_check_other}} in the required turn. Both angles in {{worked_check_answers}} have cosine {{worked_check_level}}, so ({{worked_check_a}})({{worked_check_level}})+({{worked_check_b}})={{worked_check_c}} for each. Both lie in [0,2pi). A coordinate strictly between -1 and 1 cuts the circle at two points, so these two exhaust the interval.
@end

@question {{choose_next_step_id}} | {{choose_next_step_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{choose_next_step_objective}}
@given {{choose_next_step_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which equivalent equation has cosine itself isolated?
{{choose_next_step_choices_1}}
@feedback 12 | This equation is valid: subtracting {{choose_next_step_b}} gives ({{choose_next_step_a}})cosine(theta)={{choose_next_step_rhs}}. But the nonunit multiplier {{choose_next_step_a}} remains attached. Divide both complete sides by {{choose_next_step_a}} to meet the isolation goal; the valid intermediate move is not mathematically false.
@feedback 13 | This value gives ({{choose_next_step_a}})({{choose_next_step_shifted}})+({{choose_next_step_b}})={{choose_next_step_shifted_lhs}}, not {{choose_next_step_c}}. Division must give {{choose_next_step_rhs}}/({{choose_next_step_a}}), with the divisor's sign retained.
@after {{choose_next_step_after_1}}
@wrong Distinguish a solution-preserving intermediate equation from one that also completes the stated goal.
@why Subtract {{choose_next_step_b}} from both sides: ({{choose_next_step_a}})cosine(theta)={{choose_next_step_c}}-({{choose_next_step_b}})={{choose_next_step_rhs}}. Divide by the nonzero {{choose_next_step_a}}: cosine(theta)={{choose_next_step_rhs}}/({{choose_next_step_a}})={{choose_next_step_level}}. This has coefficient 1 on cosine. Multiplication by {{choose_next_step_a}} followed by addition of {{choose_next_step_b}} recovers the original, and ({{choose_next_step_a}})({{choose_next_step_level}})+({{choose_next_step_b}})={{choose_next_step_c}} checks the required coordinate.
@end

@question {{explain_step_id}} | {{explain_step_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{explain_step_objective}}
@given {{explain_step_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate. Alpha names the given known angle; expressions differing by a full turn name the same direction.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which reflection preserves the stated cosine coordinate?
{{explain_step_choices_1}}
@feedback 11 | A half-turn takes {{explain_step_known}} to {{explain_step_halfturn}}. It negates both coordinates, giving cosine {{explain_step_opposite}} instead of {{explain_step_level}}.
@feedback 12 | Reflecting across the vertical axis sends the known angle to {{explain_step_other_reflection}} and negates its horizontal coordinate. Its cosine is {{explain_step_opposite}}, not {{explain_step_level}}.
@after {{explain_step_after_1}}
@wrong Use the coordinate definition to decide which axis reflection keeps cosine unchanged.
@why Cosine is the horizontal coordinate. Reflection across the horizontal axis changes only the other coordinate, so cosine(2pi-alpha)=cosine(alpha). With alpha={{explain_step_known}}, the calculation is 2pi-{{explain_step_known}}={{explain_step_reflected_raw}}; adding {{explain_step_reflection_shift}} gives {{explain_step_reflected}} in the required turn, and its cosine is {{explain_step_level}}. The two proposed alternatives have coordinate {{explain_step_opposite}}. The stated coordinate is nonzero, so neither alternative can preserve it.
@end

@question {{repair_error_id}} | {{repair_error_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{repair_error_objective}}
@given {{repair_error_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate. L1-L3 are a deliberately incorrect attempt, not accepted working.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which line is the first invalid step in the displayed attempt?
{{repair_error_choices_1}}
@feedback 12 | L1 is valid isolation: ({{repair_error_c}}-({{repair_error_b}}))/({{repair_error_a}})={{repair_error_level}}. It states a coordinate equation, not a completed interval solution list. The list has not yet been checked there.
@feedback 13 | L3 endorses the list already written in L2. It is also false, but the first failure is L2's inclusion of an angle outside the domain.
@after {{repair_error_after_1}}
@wrong Read the attempt in order. A later endorsement does not move the first error to a later line.
@why L1 follows by subtracting {{repair_error_b}} and dividing by the nonzero {{repair_error_a}}. L2 is the first list and includes 2pi, which fails theta<2pi. L3 merely endorses that already invalid list. Only the line number has been identified; the corrected set still has to be selected.
@step 20 | Which corrected set contains every permitted solution?
{{repair_error_choices_2}}
@feedback 21 | This set omits {{repair_error_omitted}}. That angle has cosine {{repair_error_level}}, gives ({{repair_error_a}})({{repair_error_level}})+({{repair_error_b}})={{repair_error_c}}, and is in [0,2pi). Even an empty set can miss a valid solution.
@feedback 22 | This retains 2pi, the excluded right endpoint. Its original left side is {{repair_error_endpoint_lhs}}; regardless of whether that equals {{repair_error_c}}, theta<2pi is still required.
@after {{repair_error_after_2}}
@wrong Remove the out-of-domain angle without losing any angle that satisfies both the equation and the interval.
@why Isolation gives cosine(theta)=({{repair_error_c}}-({{repair_error_b}}))/({{repair_error_a}})={{repair_error_level}}. The allowed angles are {{repair_error_answers}}. Each has that coordinate and checks ({{repair_error_a}})({{repair_error_level}})+({{repair_error_b}})={{repair_error_c}}. In x squared+y squared=1, fixing one coordinate at {{repair_error_level}} leaves the other coordinate squared equal to 1-({{repair_error_level}}) squared; there are {{repair_error_count}} circle points. All {{repair_error_count}} are represented once in the interval, while 2pi is excluded.
@end

@question {{independent_id}} | {{independent_title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{independent_objective}}
@given {{independent_given}}
@domain Theta is real, measured in radians, with 0<=theta<2pi. Cosine is the unit-circle horizontal coordinate.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | Which set is exactly the complete solution set?
{{independent_choices_1}}
@feedback 11 | These angles have cosine {{independent_contrast}}. The original left side is ({{independent_a}})({{independent_contrast}})+({{independent_b}})={{independent_contrast_lhs}}, not {{independent_c}}. They solve a different coordinate equation.
@feedback 13 | This set omits {{independent_omitted}}, which is in the interval and has cosine {{independent_level}}. Substitution gives ({{independent_a}})({{independent_level}})+({{independent_b}})={{independent_c}}. A complete set must include that angle.
@after {{independent_after_1}}
@wrong Check the proposed angles against the original equation, both endpoints, and the number of possible unit-circle points.
@why Subtract {{independent_b}} on both sides to get ({{independent_a}})cosine(theta)={{independent_c}}-({{independent_b}})={{independent_rhs}}; divide by {{independent_a}} to get cosine(theta)={{independent_level}}. The complete set is {{independent_answers}}. Every listed angle checks ({{independent_a}})({{independent_level}})+({{independent_b}})={{independent_c}} and lies in [0,2pi). Fixing this coordinate in x squared+y squared=1 gives {{independent_count}} circle points: two when its magnitude is below 1, one at magnitude 1. We have exactly that many distinct directions; adding full turns cannot add another angle in this interval.
@end
